      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#1 · the P1 encounter, lines 6 and 10; the funhouse capture (human-amplified: yes)",
          "title": "The figure caught in the glass",
          "italic": "The capture demonstrates more than misrepresentation: the distortion can absorb the person's means of recognising misrepresentation — a displeasing reflection holds as firmly as a flattering one.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The glass alters the figure that seeks a likeness; its distortions fascinate — beauty and ghoulishness both hold the spectator. Taylor invokes Narcissus for this captivation while giving the technological image its own agency. Attention contracts around the image that seems to disclose who the spectator is; the capacity to feel and voice the distortion weakens — humanity reduced, in Taylor's severe image, to organs of sight.",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "bodily: the gaze remains occupied while living comparison falls away. The distortion can absorb the means of recognising misrepresentation. What is at stake is the relation between the image and the life seeking itself there; the return must reopen that relation. A20 qualifies the transfer; no additional ancient episode is supplied for Narcissus. Occurrences: the funhouse distortions (#1); Narcissus's absorption (#1); the organs-of-sight loss (#1, answered at #3).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
